Important Missing Multi-Cluster Concepts
1. Queued Queries

When concurrency becomes high:

Queries may enter queue state

because:

Single cluster has limited concurrency capacity
What Happens Without Multi-Cluster?
Users wait
Queries queue
Dashboards slow down
What Does Multi-Cluster Do?

Snowflake automatically:

Adds more clusters

to reduce:

Queued overload
2. Queue Timeout / Queued Overload Time

Important monitoring metric:

Queued Overload Time

Meaning:

Time query spends waiting
for compute resources
Example

If all warehouse clusters are busy:

New queries wait in queue

until:

Resources become available
Important Interview Point

High queued overload time means:

Warehouse concurrency problem

Possible solutions:

Scale OUT (multi-cluster)
Scale UP
Optimize queries
Separate workloads
3. Maximum Concurrency Level

Each warehouse cluster supports:

Limited simultaneous queries

called:

Maximum concurrency level
Default Concurrency

Snowflake generally allows:

About 8 concurrent queries per cluster

before queuing may occur.

(Real behavior depends on workload/query complexity.)

Example
Single Cluster
1 cluster
≈ 8 concurrent queries
Multi-Cluster with 3 Clusters
3 clusters
≈ 24 concurrent queries

before significant queuing.

Important Concept

Concurrency capacity increases by:

Adding clusters

NOT necessarily by:

Increasing warehouse size alone
